\chapter{Currency and retraction checks}
\label{crossref-module}

A citation can decay after it is made.  The cited work may be retracted, a
correction or erratum may change its conclusions, or---common for
preprints---a peer-reviewed version may supersede the copy that was found.
A claim-backing workflow must not rest on withdrawn or stale work, so we need a
way to ask, for a paper we already hold, \enquote{is this still safe to cite?}

This chapter answers that question against Crossref.  When a publisher issues a
retraction or correction, the affected work's Crossref record gains an
[[update-by]] (rendered [[updated-by]] in the JSON) entry pointing at the notice;
and Crossref has, since ingesting the Retraction~Watch database, populated these
for tens of thousands of works regardless of publisher participation.  A single
metadata lookup per DOI therefore covers both the issue's core ask (retractions
and corrections) and the optional Retraction~Watch cross-check, with no API key.

\section{Design overview}

The check is one HTTP request per paper: [[GET https://api.crossref.org/works/
{doi}]].  Two parts of the returned [[message]] interest us:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[updated-by]] ] A list of retraction/correction notices that target
  this work.  Each entry carries the notice's DOI, an update [[type]]
  (\enquote{retraction}, \enquote{correction}, \enquote{erratum}, \dots), a
  human label, a [[source]] (e.g.\ \enquote{retraction-watch}), and a date.
\item[ [[relation]] ] A map of typed relationships to other works.  We read the
  ones that imply a newer canonical version exists, so a superseded preprint can
  be flagged.
\end{description}

We deliberately query the work \emph{itself} rather than searching Crossref for
notices that reference it.  The [[updated-by]] back-link is already present on
the work's own record, so the direct lookup is both simpler and exact---no
query parsing, no ranking, no risk of matching the wrong work.

Two design choices mirror the existing providers (\cref{cache-module} and the
provider chapter) so this module behaves like the rest of the system:
\begin{description}
\item[Caching] Results are cached per DOI through the shared cache layer, so
  re-verifying a session or re-opening it costs nothing.
\item[Silent failure] Network and parse errors are logged and turned into a
  \enquote{could not determine} result rather than an exception, matching the
  provider contract that callers never have to wrap lookups in [[try]].
\end{description}

A subtle but important consequence of silent failure is that we must
distinguish \enquote{checked, found nothing} from \enquote{could not check}.
Only an HTTP~200 response means we actually saw the work's record; a 404
(DOI not in Crossref), a rate-limit, or a transport error tells us nothing about
its currency.  We return a result dictionary only for the former and [[None]]
for the latter, so a transient failure never masquerades as a clean bill of
health.

