\section{The Crossref client}

The client encapsulates the HTTP session, the polite-pool identity, the
per-DOI cache, and proactive rate limiting.  We model it on
[[SemanticScholarProvider]] so that a reader who knows one knows the other; the
shared [[load_cache]]/[[register_cache]] calls even let it reuse the same
on-disk cache machinery.

The polite identity comes from an email address: Crossref offers a faster,
more reliable \enquote{polite} request pool to clients that identify themselves
with a [[mailto]].  We take it from the shared [[contact_email]] resolver
(\cref{sec:contact-email}), so the one [[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]] setting serves
Crossref, OpenAlex, and Unpaywall alike, while a dedicated [[CROSSREF_MAILTO]]
override is still honoured.

<<the crossref client>>=
class CrossrefClient:
    """Looks up retraction/currency metadata for DOIs via Crossref."""

    name = "crossref"

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self._cache: dict = load_cache(self.name)
        register_cache(self.name, self._cache)
        self._last_request_time: float = 0.0
        self._session = self._create_session()
        self._mailto = contact_email("CROSSREF_MAILTO")

    <<crossref client methods>>
@

The session carries the same exponential back-off as the other providers: a
retraction check that trips a transient 429 or 5xx should retry quietly rather
than report a false \enquote{could not check}.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def _create_session(self) -> requests.Session:
    """Create a session with exponential back-off retry logic."""
    session = requests.Session()
    retry = Retry(
        total=3,
        backoff_factor=1,  # 1s, 2s, 4s delays
        status_forcelist=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
        allowed_methods=["GET"],
    )
    session.mount("https://", HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry))
    return session
@

Crossref's public pool is lenient, but issuing one request per paper in a large
session can still amount to a burst.  A short minimum interval keeps us a polite
neighbour without making verification of a few dozen papers feel slow.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def _wait_for_rate_limit(self) -> None:
    """Ensure a minimum interval between Crossref requests."""
    elapsed = time.time() - self._last_request_time
    if elapsed < CROSSREF_MIN_INTERVAL:
        time.sleep(CROSSREF_MIN_INTERVAL - elapsed)
    self._last_request_time = time.time()
@

<<constants>>=
# Crossref REST endpoint for single-work lookups, and request tuning.
CROSSREF_WORKS_URL = "https://api.crossref.org/works"
CROSSREF_TIMEOUT = 30
CROSSREF_MIN_INTERVAL = 0.1  # seconds between requests (polite pacing)
CROSSREF_USER_AGENT = "scholarcli (https://github.com/dbosk/scholar)"
@

\subsection{Fetching a work's currency metadata}

The lookup is the heart of the module.  The DOI must be URL-encoded before it is
spliced into the path: DOIs routinely contain slashes (and occasionally spaces
or other reserved characters), which would otherwise be read as extra path
segments and produce a spurious 404.

The return contract is the checked/unknown distinction from the overview: a
dictionary with [[updated_by]] and [[newer_versions]] lists when Crossref served
the record (HTTP~200), and [[None]] when it did not (missing DOI, 404, 429, or
error).  A 404 is cached as [[None]] so an unregistered DOI is not retried every
run; a 429 or transport error is left uncached so a later run can try again.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def fetch_currency(self, doi: str | None) -> dict | None:
    """Return currency metadata for ``doi``, or ``None`` if undetermined.

    On an HTTP 200 response, returns a dict with ``updated_by`` and
    ``newer_versions`` lists (each possibly empty). Returns ``None`` when the
    DOI is missing, not found, rate-limited, or the request fails.
    """
    if not doi:
        return None

    cache_key = ("currency", doi)
    if cache_key in self._cache:
        logger.debug("crossref: cache hit for DOI %s", doi)
        return self._cache[cache_key]

    try:
        self._wait_for_rate_limit()
        url = f"{CROSSREF_WORKS_URL}/{quote(doi, safe='')}"
        params = {"mailto": self._mailto} if self._mailto else {}
        response = self._session.get(
            url,
            params=params,
            headers={"User-Agent": CROSSREF_USER_AGENT},
            timeout=CROSSREF_TIMEOUT,
        )

        if response.status_code == 404:
            # DOI unknown to Crossref: cache the miss to avoid re-lookups.
            self._cache[cache_key] = None
            return None
        if response.status_code == 429:
            logger.warning("crossref: rate limited for DOI %s", doi)
            return None

        response.raise_for_status()
        message = response.json().get("message", {})
        result = {
            "updated_by": _normalize_updated_by(message),
            "newer_versions": _normalize_newer_versions(message),
        }
        self._cache[cache_key] = result
        return result

    except requests.exceptions.HTTPError as e:
        logger.warning("crossref: lookup error for %s: %s", doi, e)
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning("crossref: lookup failed for %s: %s", doi, e)
        return None
@

Let's verify the happy path, the encoding requirement, and each
\enquote{undetermined} branch.  We mock the session's [[get]] so no network is
touched.

